    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Excellent BTC fit. The negative descent rate is the diamond — students must decide whether to write the rate as -3/4 (and confront the inequality flip) or as +3/4 subtracted from 1200 (no flip). Both routes work; the discussion of WHY they agree is the gold.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Altitude = 1200 - (3/4)t. Some groups write 1200 + (-3/4)t.\n- Q2: 1200 - (3/4)t >= 450. Subtract 1200: -(3/4)t >= -750. Divide by -3/4 and FLIP: t <= 1000. The balloon can descend for at most 1000 seconds (about 16 min 40 sec) before crossing the safety line.\n- Q3: 15 min = 900 seconds. Altitude = 1200 - (3/4)(900) = 1200 - 675 = 525 m. Still 75 m above the safety line. The crew called in time.\n- Q4: 1200 - 1.25t >= 450, so -1.25t >= -750, t <= 600 seconds. That's 400 seconds (about 6 min 40 sec) LESS time before crossing — a big difference.\n- Q5: Balloon 2: 900 - 0.5t >= 450, so t <= 900 seconds. Balloon 2 reaches 450 m at t = 900 s. Balloon 1 reaches it at t = 1000 s. Balloon 2 crosses first.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting to flip the inequality when dividing by -3/4. Getting t >= 1000 and concluding 'the balloon must descend AT LEAST 1000 seconds' (backwards).\n- Writing 1200 + (3/4)t >= 450 — using the wrong sign for descent. Gets a negative answer and gets confused.\n- For Q3, computing 1200 - (3/4)(15) instead of converting 15 minutes to 900 seconds.\n- For Q5, comparing the rates (3/4 vs. 1/2) and concluding 'the slower one takes longer' without checking the starting altitudes — which are different.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- One group wrote 1200 - (3/4)t >= 450 and flipped the inequality. Another wrote (3/4)t <= 750 and did NOT flip. Compare. Why do they get the same answer?\n- What is the EXACT moment t = 1000 seconds? Is the balloon at the safety line, above it, or below it?\n- In Q5, the second balloon descends slower but reaches the safety line first. Why? Where in the math does that show up?\n- If the safety altitude were 0 m (the ground), how would the inequality and answer change?",
    "extensions": "- The pilot installs a heater that briefly pushes the balloon up at 2 m/s. If the heater fires from t = 600 s to t = 660 s while the vent stays open, write a piecewise expression for the altitude during and after that minute. Does the balloon still cross the safety line at the same time?\n- Two balloons start at the same altitude (1200 m) but descend at different rates: Balloon A at 3/4 m/s, Balloon C at -1/3 m/s (the negative meaning it's actually rising slowly!). At what t are they 200 m apart? Set up and solve as an inequality.\n- The pilot wants to land EXACTLY at t = 720 seconds, hitting altitude 0. What constant descent rate is needed? Compare to the 3/4 m/s rate — faster or slower?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Altitude(t) = 1200 - (3/4)t.\n2. 1200 - (3/4)t >= 450. -(3/4)t >= -750. Divide by -3/4 and flip: t <= 1000. Balloon stays at or above safety for up to 1000 seconds (~16 min 40 s).\n3. t = 15 min = 900 s. Altitude = 1200 - (3/4)(900) = 1200 - 675 = 525 m. Above safety by 75 m.\n4. 1200 - 1.25t >= 450 gives t <= 600 s. That's 1000 - 600 = 400 seconds LESS time before crossing.\n5. Balloon 1: t <= 1000 s. Balloon 2: 900 - 0.5t >= 450 gives t <= 900 s. Balloon 2 crosses first (at 900 s).",
